---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RequestBuilder from '../../components/RequestBuilder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="REST API Request Builder" description="Build an API request visually and get ready-to-use fetch, axios, or Python requests code.">
  <RequestBuilder client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why generating code beats writing it from scratch</h2>
  <p>Converting a method, URL, headers, and body into correctly formatted client code involves small syntax details easy to get wrong by hand — a generator produces working code directly from the request definition, in whichever client library you're using.</p>

  <h2>How to use this builder</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Set the method, URL, headers, and body for your request.</li>
    <li>Choose your target: fetch, axios, or Python requests.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated code into your project.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Request Builder Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting Content-Type header</strong> when sending a JSON body — some servers require it to correctly parse the request.</li>
    <li><strong>Not handling errors in the generated code</strong> — basic generated snippets often omit error handling, which should be added for production use.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Request Builder</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly generating client code for testing a new API endpoint</li>
    <li>Converting a request definition between different client libraries</li>
    <li>Bootstrapping API integration code without writing boilerplate by hand</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>REST API Request Builder FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is the generated code production-ready?</strong> It's a solid starting point — add proper error handling, timeout configuration, and retry logic before using in production.</p>
</ToolLayout>
